@startuml crates
!theme plain
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam ranksep 50

title Crate layers — every runtime participant sits on the bus

header
<b>PLAN.</b> Built today: starling-bus, -proto, -model, -log, -server, starling.
starling-api, -net, -voice, -db, the edges and every feature are not written yet.
Nothing is wired to the bus yet, and the bus still lacks call() — ARCHITECTURE.md 6.1.
endheader

rectangle "**starling**  «binary»\ncomposition root — creates the bus\nand registers every port on it" as bin #FFF8DC

rectangle "**edges**\nstarling-rpc · starling-migrate" as edges #FDECEC

rectangle "**features**\nstarling-wasm\nstarling-feature-pchat · -log" as feats #EAF7EA

rectangle "**privileged services**\nstarling-state · starling-net\nstarling-voice · starling-db" as svc #E6F0FA

rectangle "**contract**  «traits only, no logic»\nstarling-api" as api #F5E6F5

rectangle "**domain**  «values, no mechanism»\nstarling-proto · starling-model\nstarling-crypto · starling-log" as domain #EAF0F7

rectangle "**starling-bus**  «the microkernel»\nports · lanes · envelopes\ncall() · send() · take()\n**depends on nothing**" as kernel #FFE9D6

bin --> edges
bin --> feats
bin --> svc
bin --> kernel : owns the instance

feats --> api
svc   --> api
edges --> api
api   --> domain

edges --> kernel
feats --> kernel
svc   --> kernel

domain -[#Red,dashed]-> kernel : **never** — the domain is called, it never sends

svc -[#Red,dashed]-> feats : **never** — CI asserts this
feats -[#Red,dashed]-> svc : **never** — reach it by port, not by name

note bottom of kernel
  IPC and nothing else — as QNX's microkernel
  is message passing, with the process manager
  (here ""starling-state"") a *service* on it.

  **Every** runtime participant holds a ""PortId""
  and a ""&dyn MessageBus"". Reaching another
  component means sending to its port; there is
  no second path, for features or for services.
end note

note right of domain
  The domain is the exception because it has no
  runtime presence — but "values" undersells it:
  ""dyn Permissions"" *computes* an answer.

  It is still not a bus participant, because it is
  **called, never calling**. A service that owns an
  evaluator invokes it in-process and puts the
  **result** — a ""Perm"", a ""bool"" — into a reply.
  The evaluator has no port, no inbox, and no way
  to reach anything itself.

  So permissions do not "magically" cross the bus:
  the state service carries the answer across, the
  way it carries any other value it computed.
  Enforced by ""scripts/check-crate-layering.sh"".
end note

@enduml
